      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bivalve was once called Waltersville in 1883, a boat landing for the Walter family plantation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://mdgenweb.org/wicomico/history/bivalvhx.htm",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from “bivalve,” meaning two valves, a nod to the oyster that sustained local watermen.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://mdgenweb.org/wicomico/history/bivalvhx.htm",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Yellow Brick House in Bivalve earned a spot on the National Register of Historic Places in 1978.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
